shape theory.chemistry.elements : theory.chemistry.periodic-table, theory.chemistry.properties {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// The 118 Elements as Traces Through Shell Structure.
//
// Each element is a trace: a specific Z with its ground-state
// electron configuration. The structure (shell filling rules)
// generates all of them. Listed by block:
//
// s-BLOCK (groups 1-2, filling s orbitals):
//   H  (1):  1s^1.  Unique: 1 proton, 1 electron. Simplest atom.
//   He (2):  1s^2.  Closed shell. Noble gas.
//   Li (3):  [He]2s^1.  First alkali metal.
//   Be (4):  [He]2s^2.  First alkaline earth.
//   Na (11): [Ne]3s^1.  Period 3 alkali.
//   Mg (12): [Ne]3s^2.  Period 3 alkaline earth.
//   K  (19): [Ar]4s^1.  4s fills before 3d (n+l rule).
//   Ca (20): [Ar]4s^2.
//   Rb (37): [Kr]5s^1.  Cs (55): [Xe]6s^1.  Fr (87): [Rn]7s^1.
//
// p-BLOCK (groups 13-18, filling p orbitals):
//   B-Ne (5-10): 2p filling. Establishes p-block pattern.
//   Al-Ar (13-18): 3p filling.
//   Ga-Kr (31-36): 4p filling (after 3d).
//   In-Xe (49-54): 5p filling (after 4d).
//   Tl-Rn (81-86): 6p filling (after 5d, 4f).
//   Nh-Og (113-118): 7p filling (after 6d, 5f).
//
//   Noble gases (group 18): He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn, Og.
//     Closed p-shell (or s-shell for He). Maximum coherence.
//     2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86, 118.
//   Halogens (group 17): F, Cl, Br, I, At, Ts.
//     One electron from closed shell. Maximum EA. Most reactive
//     nonmetals.
//
// d-BLOCK (groups 3-12, filling d orbitals):
//   Sc-Zn (21-30): 3d filling. First transition metals.
//   Y-Cd (39-48): 4d filling. Second transition row.
//   La,Hf-Hg (57,72-80): 5d filling. Third transition row.
//   Ac,Rf-Cn (89,104-112): 6d filling. Fourth transition row.
//
//   Anomalies from Hund's rules / half-filled stability:
//     Cr (24): [Ar]3d^5 4s^1 (not 3d^4 4s^2). Half-filled d.
//     Cu (29): [Ar]3d^10 4s^1 (not 3d^9 4s^2). Filled d.
//     Similar exceptions: Mo, Ag, Au, and others.
//     These are not violations: they confirm Hund's rules.
//     Half-filled and fully-filled subshells have extra stability
//     (maximum spin multiplicity or maximum symmetry).
//
// f-BLOCK (lanthanides and actinides, filling f orbitals):
//   Ce-Lu (58-71): 4f filling. Lanthanides.
//   Th-Lr (90-103): 5f filling. Actinides.
//
//   14 elements per row (2(2*3+1) = 14).
//   f-electrons are deeply buried: chemistry varies little across
//   the row. The lanthanide contraction: 4f electrons poorly
//   shield nuclear charge, so atoms shrink across the row.
//
// HYDROGEN (Z=1): the minimal atom.
//   1 proton + 1 electron. The simplest coherent atom.
//   Unique chemistry: can lose its electron (H+, like alkali
//   metals) or gain one (H-, like halogens). Sits in both
//   group 1 and group 17 structurally.
//
// The 118 elements are the complete canopy of stable and
// metastable atomic configurations up to Z=118. Beyond Z~172,
// the nuclear charge creates electric fields strong enough to
// spontaneously produce electron-positron pairs from the vacuum,
// making heavier atoms incoherent.
//
// Derives from: theory.chemistry.periodic-table,
//               theory.chemistry.properties
  """
}
